                <pb facs="0988" n="886" xml:id="pg886"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">119.2/3006: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-09-06T17:00:00+01:00">September 6,
                            1935—5 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 6—12:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>427. I discussed the Department’s 242, September 5, 1 p.m., with the Acting Under
                    Secretary of State today, who said the question resolved itself into two parts.
                    One, from a political angle, the presence of an American vessel in those waters
                    would be welcome; but, my impression is that this welcome was based on the
                    implications that might be attributed to the presence of an American naval
                    vessel at Aden at a time of Anglo-Italian tension. Two, from the point of view
                    of hospitality, he learned Aden would be crowded with shipping coming and going
                    and that therefore because of the draught, a cruiser would probably have to lie
                    outside where the anchorage left much to be desired. But any craft not larger
                    than a destroyer could be taken care of within the harbor.</p>
                <p>He mentioned in passing a recent report received by them that the Abyssinian
                    Government is putting up a reserve wireless station in case of breakdown of the
                    existing station.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atherton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
